In the proof of \cref{prop:polynomial-spaces}, dividing the canonical basis of $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{C}}\left(\alpha x^d\right)$ by $G(z)$ yields a basis of $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{H}^{\textnormal{-}\hspace{-0.03cm}}}(f)$, but this usually differs from the canonical basis $\{\vect{f}{k}\}_{0 \le k < d}$ (when well-defined); in fact, computing the relationship between these two bases is one of the main difficulties in proving \cref{thm:bases-proportional,thm:bases-proportional-2}. The advantage of expressing product identities in terms of the canonical basis is that for any $F \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{H}^{\textnormal{-}\hspace{-0.03cm}}}(f)$, by identifying Fourier coefficients one can write
\begin{equation} \label{eq:fourier-coeffs-basis}
    F = \sum_{k=0}^{d-1} \widehat{F}(k) \vect{f}{k},
\end{equation}
and the coefficients $\{\widehat{F}(k)\}_k$ may be objects of interest (in \cref{prop:2var-rog-ram-var} for example, they are the two Rogers--Ramanujan series from \cref{eq:rog-ram}). In particular, if $f(z)$ is a second-degree polynomial in $x$ with no zeros in $\{\textnormal{Im}\ z < -\textnormal{Im}\ \tau\}$ and $\widehat{f}(0) \not\in \left\{q^{-n} : n \ge 2\right\}$, \cref{prop:polynomial-spaces,prop:canonical-basis-vectors} imply that $\dim \mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{C}}(f) \le 1$ and $\dim \mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{H}^{\textnormal{-}\hspace{-0.03cm}}}(f) = 2$, with a basis $\{\vect{f}{0}, \vect{f}{1}\}$. So if we are given an entire function $F \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{C}}(f)$, it must be unique up to a scalar, and it is natural to express it as in \cref{eq:fourier-coeffs-basis}; this is the subject of \cref{prop:2var-rog-ram} for $f(z) = qx^2 - x$.

